Wait, `swap_remove` removes the element at index i and moves the last element to i. But I've already captured the prev/next of node i before that. After `swap_remove(i)`, the node that was at `last` is at index `i`. Its prev/next fields still point to its old neighbors (neighbors in the list, not in the Vec), so they're still valid — because it's still in the list! The only thing that changed is its index. So I just need to update the map: `self.map.insert(n.key.clone(), i)`.

Actually wait: `swap_remove` moves the last element into slot i and returns the removed element. The moved element's prev/next are unchanged and still correct since it's still in the list. The only fix-up needed is the map: the key that was at `last` is now at `i`.

