Also, I need to fix the prev/next pointers of the swapped-in node... no wait. The swapped-in node's prev/next point to its neighbors in the list, which haven't changed. But: hmm, actually wait. Let me re-verify: node at index `last` in the Vec has prev/next pointing to its list neighbors. Its list neighbors' prev/next point to `last` (its index). After the swap, the node is at index `i`, but its neighbors still point to `last`! That's the bug — the neighbors' pointers reference indices, and the node moved from `last` to `i`, so the neighbors' references to `last` are now stale.

Hmm, right. This is the classic issue with the swap-remove approach. The node's prev/next are indices. If a node moves from `last` to `i`, the nodes it's linked to (its prev and next neighbors in the list) still have pointers to `last`, and now point at the wrong node (whatever is at `last` after the Vec shrinks... actually after `swap_remove`, the Vec length is old_len - 1, so index `last` no longer exists!).

