That handles both the head/tail case (if the swapped node is now at head or tail) and the general case. Good.

Alternatively, a simpler and less error-prone approach: avoid swap_remove entirely and just `retain`/drain... no, that's O(n).

Another clean approach: don't swap at all — mark nodes as "free" and reuse free slots (free-list). That avoids index churn entirely: when removing a node, push its index onto the free list; when inserting, reuse a free index or push a new node. Indices never change, so the map stays valid. This is cleaner!

Let me consider: `nodes: Vec<Node>` plus `free: Vec<usize>`.

- Insert: take index from free.pop(), or if none, push a new node and index = nodes.len()-1.
- Remove: unlink from list, free.push(i). (The node's data can be left as-is; it's logically gone.)
- The map is always valid (keys are always at stable indices).
- Vec growth: over the lifetime of the cache, nodes.len() grows monotonically to max(len, high-water). Memory is O(total insertions) in the worst case... hmm, that's a memory concern but correctness-wise fine. In practice, with a bounded LRU cache, the high-water is bounded by the number of distinct keys ever inserted. That's typical for this kind of implementation.

Actually, wait — with the swap-remove approach, memory is O(capacity). With the free-list approach, memory is O(number of distinct keys ever seen). For an LRU cache used with many distinct keys (e.g. caching lookups on streaming data), the Vec keeps growing. Is that acceptable? The task says operations must be O(1) on average. Memory isn't specified. But a well-written implementation would use O(capacity) memory.

