```rust
fn remove_node(&mut self, i: usize) -> (K, V) {
    debug_assert!(self.nodes.get(i).is_some());
    // Unlink
    let prev = self.nodes[i].prev;
    let next = self.nodes[i].next;
    if let Some(p) = prev {
        self.nodes[p].next = next;
    } else {
        self.head = next;
    }
    if let Some(n) = next {
        self.nodes[n].prev = prev;
    } else {
        self.tail = prev;
    }

    let removed = self.nodes.swap_remove(i);
    let last = self.nodes.len(); // old len - 1
    if last != i {
        // The node that was at index `last` is now at `i`. Fix neighbors' pointers.
        let p = self.nodes[i].prev;
        let nx = self.nodes[i].next;
        if let Some(p) = p {
            self.nodes[p].next = Some(i);
        } else {
            self.head = Some(i);
        }
        if let Some(nx) = nx {
            self.nodes[nx].prev = Some(i);
        } else {
            self.tail = Some(i);
        }
        self.map.insert(self.nodes[i].key.clone(), i);
    }
    self.map.remove(&removed.key);
    (removed.key, removed.value)
}
```

Wait, `last` here: before swap_remove, nodes.len() was L, indices 0..L-1. If i == L-1, pop, no move. If i < L-1, swap_remove moves element L-1 to i; new length is L-1. After swap_remove, `self.nodes.len()` == L-1 == old last index. So `let last = self.nodes.len();` after swap gives the old index of the moved node. Condition `last != i` means a move happened (when i < L-1, i.e., L-1 != i ⇔ last != i). Correct.

